# Line endings, decided here rather than by whoever checked the tree out.
#
# core.autocrlf=true is the Windows default, and it rewrites every text file to
# CRLF on checkout - including the shell scripts. A shell script whose shebang
# reads "#!/bin/sh\r" is not a shell script to Linux: execve fails, and what
# comes back is ENOENT, "No such file or directory", naming the script that is
# plainly there. Building this tree from Windows and testing it from WSL over
# the same checkout is a thing this project actually does, so the scripts are
# pinned to LF and the question stops depending on who cloned it.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.ld   text eol=lf

# The PowerShell half is the mirror image: it is only ever run by Windows.
*.ps1  text eol=crlf
